      "writtenOn": "2022-11-11T23:45:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s very curious that we put a failing exit code in Result. Seems this error information should also be lifted into the error object to be slightly more idiomatic, but that would be a much larger change (hence +2).\n\nOne thing I\u0027m wondering - should we lift this dissection of the exit code into the exit code itself? The API of the status here seems error prone - there should be an overall way to get the status, and there should be a way to print error information, so we don\u0027t need to duplicate this for every spawned command.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 737,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 737,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "b5f7b6bb691e909d9cd41bfb76d701f18c4a2c76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f54d235c_44157046",
        "filename": "microdroid_manager/src/main.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 737,
      "author": {
        "id": 1944400
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-12T00:20:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Note that this is running the AVF app process, so it is a bit special.\n\nThe distinction between these two kinds of exit types is baked into the [AIDL service](http://cs/android-internal/packages/modules/Virtualization/virtualizationservice/aidl/android/system/virtualizationservice/IVirtualMachineCallback.aidl;l\u003d40-48;rcl\u003d401cbb57a45e94b1ce9f744e892f9effcbdcb6b0) design. If there is an exit code, whether or not it is 0, it becomes a `IVirtualMachineCallback::onPayloadFinished` call. Otherwise it is a `IVirtualMachineCallback::onError` call.\n\nMaybe we could just send the wait status directly so that the API matches unix?\n\n---\n\nRe duplication for other cases:\n\nThere is a case in this file, `apkdmverity_child.wait()?;`, that might be forgetting to check the exit code by mistake.\n\nThere is an upcoming feature in the stdlib that allows you to do `child.wait()?exit_ok()?` as a shorthand to move the exit status error cases into the Err half of Result: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/process/struct.ExitStatus.html#method.exit_ok\n\nIt prints out the raw exit status (instead of the exit code, etc), so not ideal IMO. Maybe there is a better option out there.",
      "parentUuid": "b4ab5b3c_28bcd79f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 737,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 737,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "b5f7b6bb691e909d9cd41bfb76d701f18c4a2c76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
